220:
'Faith, joy, mind, and mindfulness do not depart from the teaching of Gotama;
In whatever direction the One of broad wisdom goes, in that very direction I am inclined.'

'Being old and of feeble strength, my body does not run there;
But I go constantly by means of the movement of intention; for my mind, O Brahmin, is joined with Him.'

'Jiṇṇassa me dubbalathāmakassa' means of one who is old, aged, elderly, advanced in years, and reached the end of life. 'Dubbalathāmakassa' means of one who has weak strength, little strength, or insignificant strength—thus, 'jiṇṇassa me dubbalathāmakassa'.

'Teneve kāyo na paleti tattha' means the body cannot run, cannot walk, cannot go, and cannot advance to the place where the Buddha is—thus, 'teneve kāyo na paleti tattha'.

'Saṅkappayantāya vajāmi niccaṃ' means I go, I arrive, I transcend by means of the movement of intention, the movement of thought, the movement of knowledge, the movement of wisdom, and the movement of understanding the Four Noble Truths—thus, 'saṅkappayantāya vajāmi niccaṃ'.

In the phrase 'mano hi me brāhmaṇa tena yutto', 'mano' refers to that diverse mind, the mind that cognizes objects, the mind that compares... and so on, the mind-consciousness element (manoviññāṇadhātu) arising therefrom. 'Mano hi me brāhmaṇa tena yutto' means my mind is joined, intensely joined, and well-joined with the place where the Buddha is—thus, 'mano hi me brāhmaṇa tena yutto'. Therefore, the Elder Pingiya said:

'Being old and of feeble strength, my body does not run there;
But I go constantly by means of the movement of intention; for my mind, O Brahmin, is joined with Him.'

'Lying in the mud of sensual pleasures, trembling, I drifted from island to island;
Then I saw the Fully Enlightened One, who has crossed the flood and is free from taints.'

225:
What is one possessed of the ready wit of attainment (adhigama-paṭibhānavā)? In this teaching, a certain person has attained the four foundations of mindfulness, the four right efforts, the four bases of power, the five faculties, the five powers, the seven factors of enlightenment, the noble eightfold path, the four noble paths, the four fruits of recluseship, the four analytical insights, and the six higher knowledges. To him, the meaning is clear, the Dhamma is clear, and the language (nirutti) is clear. When the meaning is clear, the meaning appears to his knowledge; when the Dhamma is clear, the Dhamma appears to his knowledge; when the language is clear, the language appears to his knowledge. Knowledge regarding these three kinds of knowledge is called the analytical insight of ready wit (paṭibhāna-paṭisambhidā). The Blessed One is endowed with, fully endowed with, possessed of, fully possessed of, attained to, fully attained to, and has reached the perfection of this analytical insight of ready wit. Therefore, the Buddha is 'possessed of ready wit' (paṭibhānavā). For one who has no learning, no inquiry, and no attainment, what would appear to his knowledge? Thus, the Buddha is 'free from barrenness and possessed of ready wit.' Therefore, the Elder Piṅgiya said:

'I am increasingly confident, having heard the Sage's word;
The Fully Enlightened One who has removed the veil, free from barrenness, possessed of ready wit.'

'Having specially known the states that make one a higher god, He has known all, both high and low;
The Teacher who puts an end to problems for those who, being full of doubt, make their declarations.'

Regarding 'Having specially known the higher gods' (adhideve abhiññāya): there are three kinds of gods—conventional gods (sammuti-deva), gods by rebirth (upapatti-deva), and gods by purity (visuddhi-deva). What are conventional gods? Kings, princes, and queens are called conventional gods. These are called conventional gods. What are gods by rebirth? The gods of the Four Great Kings, the gods of the Thirty-three... the gods of the Brahma-world, and those gods beyond them are called gods by rebirth. These are called gods by rebirth. What are gods by purity? The Tathāgatas, the disciples of the Tathāgatas who are Arahants with taints destroyed, and the Paccekabuddhas are called gods by purity. These noble ones are called gods by purity. The Blessed One, having specially known the conventional gods as states that make one a higher god, having specially known the gods by rebirth as states that make one a higher god, and having specially known the gods by purity as states that make one a higher god—through knowing, weighing, investigating, developing, and making them clear—it is said: 'Having specially known the higher gods.'
